---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Prose from '../components/Prose.astro';
---

<Base
  title="About OmniShelf"
  description="Who makes OmniShelf, why the site exists, how the project is funded, and what it is not affiliated with."
>
  <div class="shell">
    <Prose>
      <p class="meta">About</p>
      <h1>About this project</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        OmniShelf is an independent, open-source ebook reader for Android and this site is
        its notebook. Both are maintained by a small group of people who read a lot and
        got tired of watching the reading experience degrade.
      </p>

      <h2>Who runs it</h2>
      <p>
        The project is maintained by the OmniShelf maintainers — the same people who
        write the code, answer the mail and publish these notes. There is no company
        behind it, no investor, and no advertising business attached to the application.
        The work is done in the open: every change to the reader is a public commit at
        <a href="https://github.com/tt7247129-sys/omnishelf" rel="noopener" target="_blank"
          >github.com/tt7247129-sys/omnishelf</a
        >, and the licence is the AGPLv3.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can reach a person, not a form, at
        <a href="mailto:hello@omnishelf.cc">hello@omnishelf.cc</a>. Mail is read by the
        maintainers; most messages get an answer within a few days.
      </p>

      <h2>Why this site exists</h2>
      <p>
        An app store listing has room for a feature list and nothing else. It cannot
        explain why paragraph reflow breaks citations, why a highlight that reads well in
        colour vanishes on an e-ink screen, or why a page-turn gesture that works on a
        tablet fails on a phone held in one hand. Those are the decisions that actually
        determine whether reading feels right, and they need paragraphs, not bullets.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the notes live here: long-form, specific, and written after the fact rather
        than as marketing copy before it. When we get something wrong, the correction is
        published in the same place.
      </p>

      <h2>How it is funded</h2>
      <p>
        The application is free and has no advertising, no purchases and no subscription.
        Development is unpaid work by the maintainers, and the infrastructure — a
        repository, a static site, a domain — costs very little.
      </p>
      <p>
        This website may in future carry third-party advertising to cover that small cost.
        If it does, the advertising will be clearly separated from the text, it will never
        appear inside an article, and the privacy policy will describe it before it is
        deployed. The application itself will not contain advertising under any
        circumstances; that promise is the reason the project exists.
      </p>

      <h2>What OmniShelf is not</h2>
      <p>
        It is not the official Librera app. OmniShelf began as a fork of the open-source
        Librera Reader project, and the debt is acknowledged in the app's notice file and
        in the repository. It is not affiliated with, endorsed by or sponsored by
        Librera's author.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is also not connected to any other company, product or service that happens to
        use the name OmniShelf. The name was chosen for this project; any similarity to an
        unrelated product is coincidental and does not imply a relationship.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        These notes make technical claims. If one of them is wrong — a rendering
        behaviour, a file-format detail, a statement about how another reader works —
        write to <a href="mailto:hello@omnishelf.cc">hello@omnishelf.cc</a> with the
        specific sentence. Corrections are published with a visible note rather than
        edited away silently.
      </p>
    </Prose>
  </div>
</Base>
